# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy to .env and fill in real values.  NEVER commit .env (it is gitignored).
#
# Rule of thumb:
#   - NEXT_PUBLIC_*  -> shipped to the browser. Only ever put non-secret,
#                       already-public values here (a public URL, a public
#                       anon key). Anything here is world-readable.
#   - everything else -> server-only. Never referenced from a "use client"
#                        component. These are the values that must be rotated
#                        if they ever leak. See SEGURIDAD.md for the procedure.
#
# On Vercel these are set per environment (Production / Preview / Development)
# in Project Settings -> Environment Variables, NOT in this file. Preview and
# Production must use SEPARATE database + secret values. See SEGURIDAD.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Database (server-only) ------------------------------------------------
# Postgres connection strings (Neon). Required — Vercel's serverless functions
# have a read-only filesystem, so a local SQLite file can't be written to.
#   DATABASE_URL          pooled (PgBouncer) connection, used by the app at runtime.
#   DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED direct connection, used only for running migrations.
# The password in these strings is a secret: rotate it in the Neon dashboard
# if the value is ever exposed.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@host-pooler/dbname?sslmode=require"
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED="postgresql://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require"

# --- Auth (server-only) ---------------------------------------------------
# Long random string used to pepper OTP and IP hashes.
# Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Rotating this value invalidates every pending password-reset code.
AUTH_SECRET=""

# Independent JWT signing secret. Rotating it closes every active session.
# Generate with the same command used for AUTH_SECRET.
JWT_SECRET=""

# bcrypt work factor. Production minimum: 10.
BCRYPT_SALT_ROUNDS="10"

# --- Error monitoring (server-only) ---------------------------------------
# HTTPS webhook that receives JSON events from app/global-error.tsx.
ERROR_LOG_WEBHOOK_URL=""

# --- Facturación electrónica (server-only) ---------------------------------
# Encrypts the digital certificate, its password and the OSE API credentials
# each restaurant stores in Configuración > Facturación (AES-256-GCM).
# Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# If it is left empty the key is derived from AUTH_SECRET, which works but ties
# the two together: rotating AUTH_SECRET would make every stored certificate
# unreadable. Set this before a real restaurant uploads one, and rotate it on
# its own afterwards.
BILLING_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""

# --- Trabajos de fondo (server-only) ---------------------------------------
# Secreto que autoriza las rutas de cron (/api/cron/billing/*): reintentos de
# emisiones fallidas, envío de comprobantes por correo y respaldo de CDRs.
# Vercel lo manda solo como "Authorization: Bearer <valor>" en cada disparo.
# Genera uno con:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# SIN ESTE VALOR LOS JOBS NO CORREN: la ruta responde 503 en vez de quedar
# abierta a cualquiera que adivine la URL.
CRON_SECRET=""

# --- Respaldo de comprobantes en S3 (server-only, OPCIONAL) ----------------
# SUNAT obliga a conservar cada comprobante y su respuesta cinco años. La base
# de datos ya los guarda; esto los saca de ahí una vez al día para que un
# borrado o la pérdida de la instancia no se lleve el archivo entero.
#
# Si estas variables están vacías, el job de respaldo simplemente no hace nada
# (no es un error). El bucket DEBE ser privado: dentro van comprobantes con el
# DNI o el RUC de personas reales.
#
# Sirve cualquier S3 compatible (AWS, Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, MinIO):
# para los que no son de AWS, pon su host en S3_ENDPOINT.
S3_BUCKET_CDRS=""
S3_REGION="us-east-1"
S3_ACCESS_KEY=""
S3_SECRET_KEY=""
S3_ENDPOINT=""

# Comma-separated emails that are granted the "admin" role on login. Everyone
# else who logs in becomes a "client" (restaurant owner); waiters ("mozo") are
# added by an owner from the dashboard. Not a secret, but keep it tight.
ADMIN_EMAILS="you@example.com"

# --- Public site URL (safe to expose) -----------------------------------
# Absolute origin of the deployed site. Used for metadata / OG tags and for
# absolute links in transactional emails. No trailing slash.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# --- Identidad fiscal pública --------------------------------------------
# Estos datos se imprimen en el footer, Libro de Reclamaciones y comprobantes.
NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_NAME="Adonys Pereda"
NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_TAX_ID=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_TAX_ADDRESS="Lima, Lima, Perú"
NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_EMAIL="info@foodflow.site"
NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_PHONE="+51 950 360 685"

# Google Analytics 4 measurement id. Leave empty to disable analytics.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=""

# --- Google Maps (browser key, NOT a secret) -------------------------------
# Used only by the delivery address picker in the public ordering checkout
# (/pedido/<slug>). The script is loaded lazily, the first time a diner picks
# "Delivery" — the carta itself never requests it.
#
# This key ships in the browser, so it is public by definition. What keeps it
# from being spent by strangers is the restriction list in Google Cloud, NOT
# secrecy. Before pasting a key here, in Google Cloud Console -> Credentials:
#   1. Application restrictions -> "Websites", and list ONLY your own hosts:
#        https://foodflow.site/*        https://*.foodflow.site/*
#        http://localhost:3000/*        (development key only)
#   2. API restrictions -> "Restrict key", and tick ONLY:
#        Maps JavaScript API, Places API (New), Geocoding API
#   3. Leave every other API off. This key must never be able to reach an
#      admin, billing or Cloud-management API.
#   4. Set a daily quota cap on each of those three APIs.
# Use SEPARATE keys for production and development so revoking one does not
# take the other down. If the server ever needs a Google API, give it its own
# server-only key with IP restrictions — never reuse this one.
#
# Leave empty to ship without the map: the checkout then shows the address,
# district and reference fields on their own and orders still go through.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=""

# Optional. A cloud-styled Map ID (Google Cloud Console -> Map management)
# enables Advanced Markers. Without it the picker falls back to the classic
# marker, which drags and drops exactly the same.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_MAP_ID=""

# --- Search Console (safe to expose) ---------------------------------------
# Token de la verificación por etiqueta HTML de Google Search Console. En
# Search Console: añadir propiedad -> prefijo de URL -> "Etiqueta HTML", y
# copiar SOLO el valor del atributo content, no la etiqueta entera.
#
# Vacío no renderiza ninguna etiqueta, así que dejarlo así no rompe nada: solo
# significa que la propiedad no está verificada por esta vía. Se lee en
# app/layout.jsx (metadata.verification.google).
#
# No es un secreto — va en el HTML público y cualquiera puede leerlo. Tampoco
# da acceso a nada por sí solo: solo prueba, ante Google, que quien lo pegó
# controla el dominio.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=""

# --- Email / OTP delivery (server-only) --------------------------------
# SMTP config for sending the login codes. If left unset in development, OTP
# codes are printed to the server console instead of emailed.
#
# Using Resend: SMTP_FROM must be on a domain verified in the Resend dashboard
# (Domains -> Add Domain -> add the DNS records). Until it shows "Verified",
# Resend's sandbox only delivers to the address the Resend account signed up
# with — every other recipient silently fails.
#
# No domain yet? Gmail SMTP works as a temporary stand-in:
#   SMTP_HOST="smtp.gmail.com"
#   SMTP_PORT="587"
#   SMTP_USER="youraccount@gmail.com"
#   SMTP_PASS="<16-char Gmail App Password, NOT your normal password>"
#   SMTP_FROM="FoodFlow <youraccount@gmail.com>"   (must match SMTP_USER)
# Generate an App Password at https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
# (requires 2-Step Verification). SMTP_PASS is a secret — revoke + regenerate
# it in the Google Account security page if it ever leaks.
SMTP_HOST=""
SMTP_PORT="587"
SMTP_USER=""
SMTP_PASS=""
SMTP_FROM="FoodFlow <no-reply@foodflow.app>"
